<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<configuration>
    <include resource="org/springframework/boot/logging/logback/defaults.xml"/>

    <!--
      Single console appender for all environments. The output FORMAT is chosen by
      Spring Boot's structured logging property, `logging.structured.format.console`:
        - unset (local dev)  -> human-readable plain text
        - `ecs` (prod)       -> one-line JSON per event, which Alloy ships to Loki
      Prod sets it via LOGGING_STRUCTURED_FORMAT_CONSOLE in docker-compose.prod.yml.

      Spring Boot ships two mutually exclusive appender fragments for this -
      console-appender.xml (PatternLayoutEncoder) and structured-console-appender.xml
      (StructuredLogEncoder). Boot picks between them itself ONLY when an application has
      no logback configuration of its own. This file exists, so we have to pick, and the
      selection below is deliberately written as property substitution rather than a
      conditional. Two dead ends are worth recording, because both fail silently:

        1. `<if condition="...">` (what this file used before Spring Boot 4) is DEPRECATED
           AND IGNORED as of Logback 1.5.38, which Boot 4.1.1 manages. Neither <then> nor
           <else> runs, no CONSOLE appender is ever created, and the application starts
           perfectly and logs NOTHING. The only clue is one line in logback's own status
           output: "Appender named [CONSOLE] could not be found."
        2. Deleting this file entirely and letting Boot choose does not work either: with
           no logback config, `logging.pattern.console` is honoured but
           `logging.structured.format.console` is NOT, so prod silently drops back to
           plain text and the JSON log pipeline breaks.

      So: CONSOLE_LOG_STRUCTURED_FORMAT (defined by defaults.xml above, defaulting to the
      empty string) is used to look up a filename prefix: CONSOLE_APPENDER_PREFIX_<value>.
      A recognised structured format resolves to "structured-" and selects the structured
      fragment; anything else (including the empty default) falls through the `:-` to the
      empty string and selects the plain one. Nested substitution is a core Logback feature
      and needs neither Janino nor a custom Condition class.

      The `:-` empty default is doing real work and is not decoration: Logback REJECTS
      `<property name="..." value=""/>` outright ("either the file attribute alone, or the
      resource element alone, or both the name and value attributes must be set"), so the
      plain case cannot be expressed as a declared property and has to come from the
      substitution default instead.

      Adding a new structured format means adding one more prefix property here.
    -->
    <property name="CONSOLE_APPENDER_PREFIX_ecs" value="structured-"/>
    <property name="CONSOLE_APPENDER_PREFIX_gelf" value="structured-"/>
    <property name="CONSOLE_APPENDER_PREFIX_logstash" value="structured-"/>
    <property name="CONSOLE_APPENDER_KIND"
              value="${CONSOLE_APPENDER_PREFIX_${CONSOLE_LOG_STRUCTURED_FORMAT}:-}"/>

    <include resource="org/springframework/boot/logging/logback/${CONSOLE_APPENDER_KIND}console-appender.xml"/>

    <root level="INFO">
        <appender-ref ref="CONSOLE"/>
    </root>
</configuration>
